<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-neg-binomial-DRV" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Negative Binomial Random Variable</title>

<introduction>
 <p> 
    By completing the problems provided below, you will gain experience using the Negative Binomial random variable. You will practice: 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Identifying when the Negative Binomial distributions can be used to model a scenario.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Using the Negative Binomial probability distribution function to compute probabilities.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Using the expected value and variance formulas for a Negative Binomial random variable.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>


 <exercises xml:id="hw-neg-binom-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



<exercise label="ex-is-it-negative-binomial">
  <title>Is this Negative Binomial? </title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following random variables can be modeled using a 
      Negative Binomial distribution? Select all that apply.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <!-- CORRECT: Standard Negative Binomial (waiting for r-th success, r > 1) -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A quality control inspector tests microchips one by one from a large production line, 
          where each chip independently has a <m>4\%</m> chance of being defective. Let <m>X</m> 
          be the total number of chips inspected until the <m>3\text{rd}</m> defective chip is found.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! Each inspection is an independent Bernoulli trial 
          with a constant <m>4\%</m> probability of “success,” and we count the total trials needed to achieve 
          a fixed target of <m>r = 3</m> successes (defective chips).
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- CORRECT: Standard Negative Binomial (failures before r-th success) -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A software deployment pipeline runs automated builds that each independently succeed 
          with probability <m>0.85</m>. Let <m>X</m> be the number of builds 
          that occur to get five successful builds completed.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
Correct!  Each build can be considered an independent Bernoulli trial 
          with a constant probability of “success” (the build completes), <m>p=0.85</m>. Builds continue until exactly five are succesful, <m>r = 3</m>.

        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 1: Binomial (fixed n, counting successes) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A basketball player makes <m>78\%</m> of her free throws. Let <m>X</m> be the number of free throws she makes out of her next 
          <m>15</m> attempts. You can assume that each attempted free throw is independent 
          of all other attempts.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Here, the number of trials is fixed in advance (<m>n = 15</m>), and we are 
          counting the random number of successes. This describes a <strong>Binomial</strong> 
          random variable, not a Negative Binomial.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 2: Sampling without replacement from a small finite population -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A standard deck of <m>52</m> playing cards is shuffled well, and cards are dealt one 
          at a time without replacement. Let <m>X</m> be the number of cards dealt 
          until the second Ace appears.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Because cards are dealt without replacement, the 
          trials are not independent. The probability of drawing an Ace changes after each trial.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 3: Non-constant probability (learning/fatigue effect) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A student attempts a series of logic puzzles. On each attempt, the student learns from 
          previous mistakes, so the probability of solving a puzzle increases by <m>5\%</m> after 
          every failed attempt. Let <m>X</m> be the number of attempts needed to solve three puzzles.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. The probability of success is not constant from trial to trial because the 
          student's success rate increases after each failure!
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 4: Geometric special case (r = 1) - pedagogical check -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A geological survey team drills exploratory wells in a large basin where each well 
          independently has a <m>0.12</m> probability of striking oil. Let <m>X</m> be the number 
          of wells drilled until they strike oil for the very first time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! 
Each well drilled can be considered an independent Bernoulli trial 
          with a constant <m>12\%</m> probability of “success” (striking oil). We count the number of wells drilled until oil is found (stop after first success), 
<m>r = 1</m>.
</p>
<p>
Note: Random variable <m>X</m> can also be considered Geometric. The Geometric random variable is a special case of the Negative 
          Binomial where <m>r = 1</m> specifically.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>

  <hint>
        <p>
      To determine if a variable fits the geometric model, check if it satisfies the following assumptions:    
</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
Binary - Are there exactly two complementary outcomes for each trial (e.g., Success vs. Failure)? Bernoulli trials!
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
Independent - Does the outcome of one trial have no effect on the outcome of another?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
Constant Probability of Success - Does the probability (<m>𝑝</m>) stay constant for every single trial?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
Stop After the r-th Success - Does the variable count the trials or attempts required until the r-th success occurs (with the trials stopping at that point)?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </hint>
</exercise>





<exercise label="ex-negbin-curb-painting-interactive">
    <title>Direct Sales</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Your professor is annoyed by pushy door-to-door salespeople who walk through neighborhoods offering house painting  services. Let's say that the probability of a salesperson securing a customer agreement at any individual home is a constant <m>p = 0.10</m> (or 10%). Assume that all individual household interactions behave as completely independent trials.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task A: First sale on 10th house (Short Answer Input) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curb-first-sale">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the probability that the salesperson secures their <strong>first customer agreement</strong> exactly at the 10th house they visit. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>

            <p>Try this two ways - using the geometric distribution and the negative binomial distribution. Remember that the geometric distribution is just a special case of the negative binomial where <m>r=1</m>!
            </p>
              <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.039"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!  This calculation can be viewed in two identical ways:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>Geometric: 
                    <me>P(Y_G = 10) = (0.10) \cdot (0.90)^9  \approx 0.0387 \approx 0.039</me></p></li>
                    <li><p>Negative Binomial with <m>r = 1</m>: 
                    <me>P(Y_{NB} = 10) = \binom{10-1}{1-1}(0.10)^1(0.90)^{10-1} = 1 \cdot (0.10) \cdot (0.90)^9 \approx 0.039</me></p></li>
                </ul>
                
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        
    </task>

    <!-- Task B: 60 houses to make 7 sales (Short Answer Input) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curb-seven-sales">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the probability that it requires visiting exactly 60  houses in order to successfully close 7 painting agreements. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
         <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.017"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!  This follows a Negative Binomial model where the <m>r = 7</m>th success occurs precisely on trial <m>y = 60</m>:
                <me>P(Y = 60) = \binom{60-1}{7-1}(0.10)^7(0.90)^{60-7} = \binom{59}{6}(0.10)^7(0.90)^{53} \approx 0.017</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       
    </task>

    <!-- Task C: B vs C conceptual difference (Inline Dropdowns) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curb-conceptual-shift">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Compute the probability that the salesperson receives exactly 53 rejections  <em>before</em> the 7th painting agreement is completed.  Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            
              <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.017"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! This is identical to Part B, because 53 rejections plus 7 successful agreements equals exactly 60 total household visits.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </task>

    <!-- Task D: Expected houses for 25 sales (Short Answer Input) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curb-expected-quota">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 How many total houses do you expect the salesperson will have to visit in order to successfully accumulate a quota of exactly 25  painting agreements? Write your answer as a whole number.
            </p>
                 <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="250"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the expected value formula for Negative Binomial:
                <me>E(Y) = \frac{r}{p} = \frac{25}{0.10} = 250 \text{ houses}</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Task E: Expected houses for revenue milestone (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curb-revenue-mc">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If the salesperson gets a $250 bonus for each painting agreement secured, what is the mean number of household visits required to hit a bonus target of $3,000?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p><m>12</m> houses</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the value of <m>r</m>, the number of successful painting agreements required to hit $3,000 (<m>\frac{3000}{250} = 12</m>). You still need to calculate the expected number of total household visits required to land that many sales.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>30,000</m> houses</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. It looks like you divided the entire $3,000 revenue target by the probability parameter <m>p = 0.10</m>. Make sure to convert your revenue target to a target number of successful sales (<m>r</m>) first.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>120</m> houses</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! First, determine the number of target successes (<m>r</m>) needed to reach the financial goal:
                    <me>r = \frac{\$3000 \text{ target}}{\$250 \text{ per job}} = 12 \text{ agreements}</me>
                    Next, apply the expected value function using our parameter count of <m>r = 12</m> and success probability <m>p = 0.10</m>:
                    <me>E(Y) = \frac{r}{p} = \frac{12}{0.10} = 120 \text{ houses}</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>1.2</m> houses</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. It looks like you multiplied the target sales number of 12 by the probability parameter <m>p = 0.10</m> instead of dividing by it. The expected number of trials will always be larger than the target number of successes when <m>p \lt 1</m>.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>108</m> houses</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the number of failures before reaching the target number of successes.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-negative-binomial-airline-calls" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Reaching the Airline Reservation Office</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An automated audit of an airline reservation office establishes that during peak travel windows, the telephone lines are completely busy 60% of the time. 
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Geometric Distribution -->
    <task xml:id="task-airline-geometric-input">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Suppose you are calling the office during the peak travel window. Find the probability that it takes you exactly 4 total attempts to get through to an agent for the first time. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
        </p>
           
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin answer="0.086" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes"><numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            
            <feedback>
            <p> Correct! Treat as geometric: <me>P(Y_G=4)=(.6)^3 (.4)=.0864 </me> or as Negative Binomial with <m>r=1</m>: <me>
                        P(Y_{NB}=4)=\binom{3}{0}(.6)^3 (.4)=.0864 </me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
            </test>
        
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      <!--  <solution>
            <p>
                Let the discrete random variable <m>X</m> denote the number of attempts required to secure the first successful connection. Because we are counting trials until a single binary success occurs, <m>X</m> follows a standard Geometric distribution: <m>X \sim \text{Geometric}(p = 0.40)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating the Geometric PMF expression, <m>P(X = x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p</m>, at <m>x = 4</m>:
                <me>P(X = 4) = (0.60)^{4-1} \cdot 0.40 = (0.60)^3 \cdot 0.40 = 0.216 \cdot 0.40 = 0.0864</me>
                The probability that your first connection occurs on exactly the 4th attempt is precisely <strong>0.0864</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Negative Binomial (Total Trials) -->
    <task xml:id="task-airline-negbin-trials-input">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Now suppose that both you and a friend must each complete a separate reservation call to this office. If you take turns calling, find the probability that it takes a total of 4 tries for both of you to get through and reach agents. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin mode="string" answer="0.173" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes"><numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            
            <feedback>
            <p> Correct! Treat as Negative Binomial with <m>r=2</m>: <me>
                        P(Y_{NB}=4)=\binom{3}{1}(.6)^2 (.4)^2=.1728 \approx 0.173 </me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                Let the random variable <m>Y</m> denote the total number of combined attempts required to secure exactly <m>r = 2</m> successful connections. Because we are tracking total trials until a specific number of successes is reached, <m>Y</m> follows a Negative Binomial distribution parameterized by trials: <m>Y \sim \text{NegBinomial}(r = 2, p = 0.40)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                To hit the 2nd success on exactly the 4th try, the system must capture exactly 1 success during the first 3 tries, and then finish with a guaranteed success on the 4th try:
                <me>P(Y = y) = \binom{y-1}{r-1} p^r (1-p)^{y-r}</me>
                <me>P(Y = 4) = \binom{4-1}{2-1} (0.40)^2 (0.60)^{4-2} = \binom{3}{1} (0.16) (0.36) = 3 \cdot 0.0576 = 0.1728</me>
                The probability that a total of 4 tries is required to complete both calls is precisely <strong>0.1728</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Alternative Negative Binomial (Worded as Failures) -->
    <task xml:id="task-airline-negbin-failures-input">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Again, suppose that both you and a friend must each complete a separate reservation call to this office. Calculate the probability that you encounter a busy line exactly 3 times before both of you eventually get through to an agent. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin mode="string" answer="0.138" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes"><numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            
            <feedback>
            <p> Correct! Treat as Negative Binomial with <m>r=2</m>: <me>
                        P(Y_{NB}=5)=\binom{4}{1}(.6)^3 (.4)^2=.1382 \approx 0.138</me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <!--<solution>
            <p>
                Let the random variable <m>F</m> denote the number of busy signals (failures) encountered before securing <m>r = 2</m> active connections. Under this shifted phrasing model, if we want exactly 3 failures, the total number of trials scales directly to: <m>\text{Trials} = \text{Successes} + \text{Failures} = 2 + 3 = 5 \text{ total tries}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating the alternative shifted Negative Binomial PMF at <m>F = 3</m>:
                <me>P(F = f) = \binom{f + r - 1}{f} p^r (1-p)^f</me>
                <me>P(F = 3) = \binom{3 + 2 - 1}{3} (0.40)^2 (0.60)^3 = \binom{4}{3} (0.16) (0.216) = 4 \cdot 0.03456 = 0.13824</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the probability of enduring exactly 3 failures before completing both calls is exactly <strong>0.1382</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Long-Run Expected Moments -->
    <task xml:id="task-airline-moments-input">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the expected number of total combined calling attempts required for you and your friend to both get through. Enter your answer as an integer.
            </p>
            <p>Expected Total Calls = <fillin answer="5.00" width="8"/> </p>

        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate><test correct="yes"><numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            
            <feedback>
            <p> Correct! Using the expected value formula for a Negative Binomial with <m>r=2</m>: <me>
                        E(Y)=\frac{r}{p}=\frac{2}{.4}=5 </me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <!--<solution>
            <p>
                For a Negative Binomial process tracking total trials until <m>r = 2</m> successes are met with an individual success probability of <m>p = 0.40</m>, we apply the shortcut moment formulas:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Expected Value (Mean Total Trials):</strong>
                            <me>E(\text{Total}) = \frac{r}{p} = \frac{2}{0.40} = 5.00 \text{ attempts}</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Variance of Total Trials:</strong>
                            <me>\text{Var}(\text{Total}) = \frac{r(1-p)}{p^2} = \frac{2(0.60)}{(0.40)^2} = \frac{1.20}{0.16} = 3.75 \text{ attempts}^2</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                Rounded to two decimal places, the expected total attempts required is <strong>5.00</strong> with an underlying variance of <strong>3.75</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>
</exercise>



   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-neg-binom-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>



    <exercise label="ex-alternative-rv-transformations">
       <title>Alternative Definitions for Waiting-Time Distributions</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        Both the geometric and negative binomial random variables can be defined ed by tracking either the <em>total number of trials</em> or the <em>total number of failures</em>. Let us define the alternative frameworks:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Counting Failures: </p>
              
              <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Geometric: Let <m>X_G</m> be the number of failures before the first success occurs.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                 Negative Binomial: Let <m>X_{NB}</m> be the number of failures before the <m>r</m>-th success occurs.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            </li>
            <li><p>Counting Total Trials: </p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Geometric: Let <m>Y_G</m> be the total number of trials required to reach the first success.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Negative Binomial: Let <m>Y_{NB}</m> be the total number of trials required to reach the <m>r</m>-th success.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
              </li>

        </ul>
    </p>
</introduction>
        <!-- Task A: Linear Relationships -->
        <task xml:id="task-linear-relations">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Write an algebraic equation relating the random variables <m>X_G</m> and <m>Y_G</m>, and another equation relating <m>X_{NB}</m> and <m>Y_{NB}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
           <!-- <solution>
                <p>
                    Because total trials always equal the sum of all failures plus all successes, and the number of target successes is a fixed constant in each experiment, the linear equations are:
                    <me>X_G + 1 = Y_G \quad (\text{or } Y_G - 1 = X_G)</me>
                    <me>X_{NB} + r = Y_{NB} \quad (\text{or } Y_{NB} - r = X_{NB})</me>
                </p>
            </solution>-->
        </task>

        <!-- Task B: Alternative Geometric PMF 
